WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.669
Hi — this is a demo of the simulation

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:04.112
we put together to test our

3
00:00:04.112 --> 00:00:06.133
implementation of the Cassandra database

4
00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:07.864
engine, which I built together with

5
00:00:07.864 --> 00:00:09.308
engineer Matías Bartellone and

6
00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:11.039
engineer Iván Maximoff. What we do

7
00:00:11.039 --> 00:00:13.926
is simulate a real use case

8
00:00:13.926 --> 00:00:15.369
through our own imitation of

9
00:00:15.369 --> 00:00:16.523
Flightradar. We bring up four

10
00:00:16.523 --> 00:00:18.255
distributed nodes for our database,

11
00:00:18.255 --> 00:00:20.564
load it with airport data and with

12
00:00:20.564 --> 00:00:22.873
the flights that are about to start, and then

13
00:00:22.873 --> 00:00:25.182
each plane acts as a client

14
00:00:25.182 --> 00:00:26.914
updating its own data. This

15
00:00:26.914 --> 00:00:29.223
application we're about to see is another

16
00:00:29.223 --> 00:00:31.244
client, but one that only reads the data

17
00:00:31.244 --> 00:00:32.975
to visualise it and show it on the

18
00:00:32.975 --> 00:00:35.459
map we're looking at. We can see how

19
00:00:35.459 --> 00:00:37.602
the planes move, which means

20
00:00:37.602 --> 00:00:40.052
that every movement is the

21
00:00:40.052 --> 00:00:42.196
plane updating its position and

22
00:00:42.196 --> 00:00:44.340
other data, such as altitude or

23
00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:46.177
fuel. What this lets us

24
00:00:46.177 --> 00:00:48.321
show is that the engine we implemented

25
00:00:48.321 --> 00:00:50.464
can serve multiple clients at

26
00:00:50.464 --> 00:00:52.914
the same time, and they don't talk to a

27
00:00:52.914 --> 00:00:55.058
single node — each plane

28
00:00:55.058 --> 00:00:56.895
connects to its geographically

29
00:00:56.895 --> 00:00:58.120
closest node. We, on our

30
00:00:58.120 --> 00:01:00.264
side, are connected to a single node.

31
00:01:00.264 --> 00:01:02.714
That means the plane may be

32
00:01:02.714 --> 00:01:04.551
modifying the data on a node

33
00:01:04.551 --> 00:01:07.053
different from ours. And even so, we

34
00:01:07.053 --> 00:01:09.023
get the updated data. This is how

35
00:01:09.023 --> 00:01:11.323
we demonstrate the properties

36
00:01:11.323 --> 00:01:12.945
of the CAP theorem. First we have

37
00:01:12.945 --> 00:01:15.216
partition tolerance, since the

38
00:01:15.216 --> 00:01:17.163
database is distributed and replicated

39
00:01:17.163 --> 00:01:19.759
across the four nodes, and the system keeps

40
00:01:19.759 --> 00:01:21.705
running even when the nodes work

41
00:01:21.705 --> 00:01:23.003
independently. We have strong

42
00:01:23.003 --> 00:01:25.274
consistency, since data propagates

43
00:01:25.274 --> 00:01:26.896
between nodes within

44
00:01:26.896 --> 00:01:28.194
milliseconds, and when we query

45
00:01:28.194 --> 00:01:29.816
we get up-to-date data. And we achieve

46
00:01:29.816 --> 00:01:31.763
a medium-high availability —

47
00:01:31.763 --> 00:01:33.385
because the system always answers

48
00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:35.656
its clients, even if in some

49
00:01:35.656 --> 00:01:37.927
particular case we may see it with a delay

50
00:01:37.927 --> 00:01:40.198
of just milliseconds. We know the three

51
00:01:40.198 --> 00:01:42.584
properties make up the CAP theorem of

52
00:01:42.584 --> 00:01:45.408
distributed systems, which states

53
00:01:45.408 --> 00:01:47.919
that you can't guarantee all three at

54
00:01:47.919 --> 00:01:49.802
100% at the same time. In our case,

55
00:01:49.802 --> 00:01:51.685
as I said, we prioritised solid

56
00:01:51.685 --> 00:01:53.255
partition tolerance, strong consistency and

57
00:01:53.255 --> 00:01:54.510
medium-high availability.

